package org.mattshoe.mtg.core

internal val setOutlawsOfThunderJunction = StorySection(
    "otj", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction",
    "A desert world nobody had ever lived on opened its doors to the Multiverse, and the worst people in every world rode in to rob each other blind.",
    listOf(
        StoryArticle(
            "otj-the-frontier", "The Frontier",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-frontier-an-empty-world", "An Empty World",
                    listOf(
                        "Outlaws of Thunder Junction came out on April 19, 2024, under the set code OTJ, with a cowboy hat for its expansion symbol. It was the one hundredth expansion Magic had ever printed. It reached MTG Arena three days earlier, on April 16, and prerelease weekends ran from April 12 to 18. It is the last set of the [Omenpath](the-omenpaths) arc, coming after [Murders at Karlov Manor](karlov-manor) and before Bloomburrow, and its story, told in short in [Thunder Junction](thunder-junction), closes the first act of the long tale that began when the Phyrexian [invasion](invasion-of-the-multiverse) ended.",
                        "Its designers built it from the top down, and they built it as a villain set first and a Western second. Mark Rosewater led the vision team. The idea was simple and a little wicked. Every world had its outlaws, and now the Omenpaths had made a road from every world to one empty desert. What would happen if the villains of the Multiverse all showed up in the same place at once, with no law to stop them and a fortune buried somewhere under the sand?",
                        "The answer was Thunder Junction, a plane of red canyons, cactus, tumbleweeds and rattlesnakes under an enormous sky. Before the paths opened, no one lived there at all. The only things standing on it older than the newcomers were a scatter of runecarved obelisks and one legendary vault that most settlers took for a story. The plane was never touched by the Phyrexian invasion. It was simply waiting, and when the doors swung open the whole Multiverse came through them looking for land, for gold, or for somebody else to take those things from.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-frontier-thunder", "Thunder",
                    listOf(
                        "The opening of the Omenpaths woke something in the plane itself. A wild magic began to crackle across the desert as blue lightning, chaotic and destructive and impossible to predict. The settlers gave it a plain name. They called it thunder, and in time the name spread from the magic to the world it came from. Within a few years they were bottling it, binding it and building it into nearly everything, from farming tools to the deadliest weapons anybody had seen, which is the line printed on the card {Quick Draw}.",
                        "Thunder weapons come in every size a frontier could want. Thunder bows are heavy, well balanced crossbows. Thunder pistols are small and surprisingly heavy in the hand. Thunder rifles are what a sharpshooter carries, and lawmen bind their prisoners with thundercuffs, which according to {Shackle Slinger} work just as well thrown at a man's head as clapped on his wrists. People who fire them for a living are called thunderslingers. In Omenport there is even a shooting contest called the Thunder Trial, reported by the Breaking News printing of {Clear Shot}.",
                        "The weather has thunder of its own. Winter on Thunder Junction is thunderstorm season, and the desert rain comes down hard and sudden, enough to put out a burning building in a matter of minutes. The settlers also learned to harvest the sun. Newly built relays drink in the desert light and carry power to settlers across the plane, and {Conduit Pylons} shows them standing against the sky. Nothing on the plane governs any of this. There is no king and no council, only whoever has the most guns in a given town on a given day.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The opening of the Omenpaths awoke the wild magic of Thunder Junction. Settlers dubbed it \"thunder\" and put it to use in everything from farming tools to deadly weapons.",
                        "Quick Draw",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-frontier-towns", "Towns and Trails",
                    listOf(
                        "Only two places on Thunder Junction are big enough to call cities. Omenport grew up in the desert where an unusual number of Omenpaths open close together. It is a town of wooden buildings with faded paint and triangular rooftops, the triangle echoing the shape of the paths themselves, with clusters of cactus between the houses. An enchanted fountain in the plaza bubbles with cool water all day, mission bells ring at sundown, and Ral Zarek's relay tower climbs above the rooftops. A rail line called the Omenport Express runs from there to the other city.",
                        "That city is Prosperity, the richest place on the plane and the Sterling Company's in all but name. Tall gray boulders stand among its streets like skyscrapers, its buildings are clean and whitewashed, and ornate trains run on raised tracks through its heart, flanked by two long roads paved in white stone and patrolled by Sterling guards. It has Prosperity Station, a newspaper called the Prosperity Post, plenty of saloons, and the Voyager Grande, a ten story hotel, casino and entertainment palace with windmills on its roof. Its tenth floor is for the most exclusive guests, and its basement belongs to a gang.",
                        "Everything else is small. Rustwood is a failed ranching town, nearly abandoned, where the Wildcard Saloon became a hideout for thieves. Saddlebrush is a quiet, isolated place ringed with badlands and dunes. Hardbristle is a settlement of cactusfolk along a train line near the hills, and {Hardbristle Bandit} warns that its people are prickly. Ironstone is a roadside stop with one card den, an abandoned mine and a rail station. Hidden in a circular canyon, under a floating mountain that spills lava into its streets, lies Tarnation, the town nobody is supposed to know about.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-frontier-wild-country", "The Wild Country",
                    listOf(
                        "Past the last fence post the land belongs to no one. Blacksnag Bog is a treacherous swamp crossed by an old miners' trail, and {Treasure Dredger} says the trail is littered with lost fortunes and the bones of the people who went looking for them. Fool's Fall is the headwater of a river, watched over by strange snakes and by a djinn, and {Djinn of Fool's Fall} admits that nobody knows whether he guards the waterfall or conjured it to hide something greater. There are geysers where drakes dive through the scalding water in mating season, and where a sphinx was once seen among the steam.",
                        "Prospectors bury their own at Thief's Folly, a walled necropolis of tombs and mausoleums among long dead trees. Its graves are a patchwork of dozens of worlds, each marker written in the way of the dead person's home plane with one line of Thunder Junction's own simplified script beneath it, and small sigils etched into the corners forming a quiet incantation. Some bodies were carried there from other planes. Travellers also trade stories of the Eversaloon, a refuge that appears at the very moment it is needed most, and nobody can say whether it is another world through an Omenpath or a pocket of this one.",
                        "The wildlife is all frontier. There are rattlewurms big enough to threaten a town, such as {Colossal Rattlewurm}, armadillos, coyotes and roadrunners, jackalopes, prairie dogs, varmints that eat anything they can fit in their mouths, and holy cows that are part angel. Horses, gila monsters, bears, beavers and even newts carry riders, and almost anything with four legs and a temper can be saddled. Not all the newcomers adjusted well. With almost no mammals around, some vampires tried feeding on cactusfolk, and the flavor of {Desperate Bloodseeker} says how that went for everyone involved.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "With no mammals around, some vampires tried to feed on cactusfolk, with horrible results for everyone involved.",
                        "Desperate Bloodseeker",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-frontier-newcomers", "Who Came Through",
                    listOf(
                        "Every sapient being on Thunder Junction came from somewhere else, with one exception. The cactusfolk are the only people known to be native to the plane, walking plants with spines and a talent for growing things in places nothing should grow. Everyone else arrived through the Omenpaths in the few years after the invasion. The Riveteers of New Capenna laid down the railroads. Ral Zarek came from Ravnica to build the relay towers. The nomadic Atiin, a people from a plane the story never names, arrived in numbers, and many of the plane's best riders and shooters wear their beadwork.",
                        "The famous followed the poor. From Avishkar came Kambal, once a corrupt consul of Ghirapur, and the aetherborn crime lord Gonti. From Fiora came Queen Marchesa, Grenzo and Selvala. From Innistrad came the vampire queen Olivia Voldaren, the necromancers Gisa and Geralf, the stitched horror Kraum and the Gitrog monster. Kaldheim sent the dwarf raider Magda, Tarkir sent the goblin Vial Smasher, Zendikar sent Bruse Tarl and Ertha Jo, Shandalar sent Riku and Talrand, and Ravnica sent the shapeshifter Lazav, the demon Rakdos and the gorgon Vraska.",
                        "Some came by accident. {Fblthp, Lost on the Range} is the same unlucky homunculus who has been lost his whole life, and the Omenpaths simply gave him more places to be lost in. Others came on purpose and meant harm. The newcomers made the plane's culture out of their own. Whiskey, birch beer and cocktails are poured in every saloon, cards and dice are everywhere, piñon nuts are a common snack, and a peddler in Omenport will try to sell you herbs from Amonkhet and cookware from Fiora in the same breath.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Can I interest you in some herbs from Naktamun? Fresh from the Omenpath! Or a set of Fioran cookware? Finest in the Multiverse!",
                        "Discerning Peddler",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction"),
                StorySource("Story", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction, Episode 1, An Offer of Revenge, by Akemi Dawn Bowman, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Story", "No Tells, by Isaac Fellman, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Story", "A Pleasant Family Outing, by Seanan McGuire, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Outlaws of Thunder Junction"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Thunder Junction"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "otj-the-gangs", "The Gangs",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-gangs-hellspurs", "The Hellspurs",
                    listOf(
                        "The Hellspurs are the most ferocious gang on Thunder Junction, and their colors are black and red. They are the plane's hardest, most violent bandits, and they take the land's wild magic straight into their own bodies. Their ranks hold cactusfolk, giants, humans, minotaurs, orochi, skeletons, zombies and lizardfolk, some with flaming hair, some with skin of running lava, some hidden behind iron masks. They dress in black and red cloth, use thunder freely, and reach for fire magic and necromancy when thunder is not enough.",
                        "Their leader was {Akul the Unrepentant}, a scorpion dragon from the plane of Gastal. He had golden eyes, mandibles in front of two rows of teeth, chitinous claws and a barbed tail he could crack like a whip, and the scales on his chest glowed with thunder when he was angry. His venom could kill in seconds, and anyone who survived it lived in pain for the rest of their life. Around him stood an inner circle of at least seven, including Twist Fandango, and in the wider gang rode the goblin {Vial Smasher, Gleeful Grenadier}, the lizard {Laughing Jasper Flint}, a vampire called Frankie the Fang, Gorra Tash and Silas, and Eriana.",
                        "The Hellspurs built their own town. Tarnation was raised around the ancient vault at the center of the plane, which Akul wanted more than anything and could not open. Only Hellspurs were allowed inside, arguments were settled with duels in the street, and the town's location was kept secret in the simplest way available, by killing every outsider who learned it. When the Freestriders once robbed the gang, Akul hunted them for weeks. When the survivors gave the money back, he struck them anyway.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Crackling with rage and stolen thunder, he drives the Hellspurs to ever-greater heights of violence.",
                        "Akul the Unrepentant",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-gangs-freestriders", "The Freestriders",
                    listOf(
                        "The Freestriders ride in green and white. They are nomads and vigilantes who do what they believe is right whatever the law happens to say, and they believe in liberation in all its many forms, which is the line on {Requisition Raid}. They live off the land, go where the wind takes them, and help the people nobody else will help along the way. Their badge is half a sunburst, usually worn on the shoulder.",
                        "A good deal of their work happens at the Omenpaths. New arrivals step through into a strange desert with everything they own on their backs, and robbers and charlatans wait there for easy marks. The Freestriders took to patrolling near the paths to protect them, as {Omenport Vigilante} records. Their rule of thumb is simple. If the law and what is right disagree, they ride with what is right.",
                        "Their riders include the elf {Selvala, Eager Trailblazer}, who came from Fiora, the druid {Miriam, Herd Whisperer}, who talks to animals and demands respect for any beast that will carry you across the sand, {Wylie Duke, Atiin Hero}, Bruse Tarl of Zendikar, Betty Wray and Miron Tillas Jr. Annie Flash rode with them once, and the gang's history is tied to hers. It was the Freestriders who robbed the Hellspurs, and it was a Freestrider's nephew who paid for it when Akul came for them.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Anything willing to bear your weight and save your feet a trip across the hot sands deserves respect. Show some.",
                        "Miriam, Herd Whisperer",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-gangs-outcasters", "The Outcasters",
                    listOf(
                        "The Outcasters are green and blue, and they are barely a gang at all. They are scattered groups of hermits and outlaws who huddle in little ramshackle towns across the plane, survivalists who keep close to nature and help the land grow. They prefer solitude and find endless wonder in the natural world, as {Map the Frontier} puts it, and they welcome any pioneer who has had enough of crowds and cities. Their rule is short. If you do not bother them, they will not bother you.",
                        "You can tell them by their clothes, made from what the desert gives. Heavy fur shoulder pads and cloaks, fur lined armor, plates of cactus, horns and spikes. They often travel with loyal animal companions, and some explore unmapped country or run experiments on the plants and beasts they find. One of them, a scholar of ancient artifacts named Nolan, knew enough about old relics that the Sterling Company hired him to study the key to the vault, and that made him the man every gang on the plane suddenly wanted to meet.",
                        "Their best known members are scholars and giants. {Doc Aurlock, Grizzled Genius} is a bear druid who believes learning stops only when you close your mind to wonder. {Bonny Pall, Clearcutter} is a giant who travels with Beau, an ox who on the card grows with every land you control. {Outcaster Greenblade} shows a swordsman in whose shade the desert blooms. Next to the Hellspurs and the Sterling Company they look harmless, and that suits them fine.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Learning stops only when you close your mind off to wonder.",
                        "Doc Aurlock, Grizzled Genius",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-gangs-slickshots", "The Slickshot Gang",
                    listOf(
                        "The Slickshot Gang wear blue and red, and purple when they can get it. They are outlaws and thunderslingers for hire, and they pride themselves on being the best shots and the quickest draws on the plane. They do everything with style, from fanned coat flaps and elaborate collars to the way they stage a duel. Every duel is a chance for pageantry, and every con is a shot at fame. They are meant to have each other's backs, so a wrong done to one Slickshot is a wrong done to all of them, though in practice most of them work alone.",
                        "Their boss is {Lilah, Undefeated Slickshot}, a human crime boss who founded the gang soon after the paths opened and recruited outlaws from many worlds. Part of their success came from an elixir only Lilah knew how to brew, a drink that strengthened its drinker's magic, and she shared it only with her own people. Their headquarters sits in the basement of the Voyager Grande in Prosperity. Baron Graywater calls them cheap tricksters and charlatans in the flavor of {Rodeo Pyromancers}, which tells you how well they get along with the law.",
                        "The gang's ranks include the ogre chronologist {Obeka, Splitter of Seconds}, who breaks the flow of time by hitting it, the elemental Eris, the knife thrower {Stella Lee, Wild Card}, a thief called Paq, and others named Tyron, Squeakers and Lucy Bluff. Their cards favor casting two spells in a turn, a gunslinger's double tap, and creatures like {Slickshot Show-Off} who get stronger with every trick they pull.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "I didn't miss. Blasted thing snatched my bolt out of the air and ate it!",
                        "Lilah, Slickshot boss, Magebane Lizard",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-gangs-sterling", "The Sterling Company",
                    listOf(
                        "The Sterling Company wears white, gray and silver with silver filigree, and its colors on the card table are white and black. It is a private peacekeeping force founded by the vampire {Baron Bertram Graywater}, and it is the closest thing Thunder Junction has to law. Graywater wrote the plane's laws himself, and he makes no secret of whose side they are on. The company believes money and a firm hierarchy are the only way to bring order to the chaos, and anyone who will not fit into that order is banished.",
                        "There is only one law that truly matters to the Sterling Company, says {Ruthless Lawbringer}, and it is to never stand in the way of profit. The company hires out guards to anyone who can pay and pays its own mercenaries well. Ral Zarek hired them to protect his relay towers. Its headquarters stands at the edge of Prosperity behind a metal fence flickering with blue energy, a hall of white pillars under a staggering glass ceiling, with a front desk of solid marble surrounded by pots of trimmed cactus. In the basement is a jail, and illusions make its cells look empty even when they are full.",
                        "The mayor of Prosperity is a company man. {Kambal, Profiteering Mayor} once oversaw the distribution of aether in a city on Avishkar and lost that post after a revolution, and he found a new office waiting for him on the frontier. In development the company was called the Silver Star gang, and it owes a good deal to the private detective agencies of the old American West. Not everyone loves it. A townsperson on {Vengeful Townsfolk} asks where the Sterling Company was when the Hellspurs burned half the town, and answers the question himself.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Of course the law's on my side. I wrote it.",
                        "Baron Bertram Graywater",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction"),
                StorySource("Story", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction, Episodes 1 to 6, by Akemi Dawn Bowman, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Story", "A Long Way From Home, by Akemi Dawn Bowman, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Hellspurs"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Freestriders"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Outcasters"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Slickshot Gang"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Sterling Company"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "otj-the-crew", "Oko's Crew",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-crew-ringleader", "The Ringleader",
                    listOf(
                        "Oko is a fae shapeshifter and one of the few planeswalkers who kept his spark when almost everyone else lost theirs, the event told in [Sparks Lost](sparks-lost). He can wear any face he likes and turn a man into an elk if he is in the mood. Somebody calling themselves Ashiok hired him for a job on Thunder Junction, to break open the legendary vault at the plane's center, and Oko set about building a crew the way a card sharp builds a hand, choosing every piece for what it could do and caring about none of them.",
                        "He had a son he had never met. Kellan, a half fae boy from Eldraine, had been crossing world after world looking for his father, and the moment the two of them saw each other in the street the shock of it broke Oko's disguise. Oko let the boy join the gang because loyalty offered that freely looked useful. Over the course of the job he lied to him, left him on a runaway train, and chose him for the riskiest part of the plan precisely because he was such a poor liar that he was sure to get them all caught.",
                        "On the card {Oko, the Ringleader} he becomes a copy of one of your creatures each combat, and his abilities draw cards, make elk and finally copy everything you have, a ringleader who is whatever the job needs. He is also on the Collector Booster box, squaring off against his son. When the heist was over he was nearly turned to stone by his own second in command and planeswalked away with nothing. He left his son's life to his son.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-crew-the-kid", "The Kid",
                    listOf(
                        "Kellan grew up in Orrinshire on Eldraine, mocked by the other children for his fae blood, and became a hero there on the quest to end the curse of the Wicked Slumber, the story told in [Wilds of Eldraine](wilds-of-eldraine). He has no spark. He crossed to Ixalan and then to Ravnica on foot through the Omenpaths, and on Ravnica he learned his father was on Thunder Junction. He signed on as a laborer building Ral Zarek's relay tower at Omenport, and after he caught a worker falling from the scaffold, Ral made him a bodyguard instead.",
                        "In the gang they called him the Kid, a nod to Billy the Kid, and {Kellan, the Kid} gives him flying and lifelink and a knack for turning a spell cast from anywhere but your hand into a free one. He was too honest for the work. He was too polite to a Hellspur and started a brawl, and he refused to leave a train full of strangers to die. Twice during the job his fae magic woke more fully, once when he put Akul into a trance in a duel to the death, and once in the collapsing vault, when he took the dragon's mind completely and walked him back into the dark.",
                        "His father used him and walked away from him, and Kellan came out of it knowing who he was. He stayed on Thunder Junction, turned down a job on Ravnica, and sent a message through the relay tower to Amalia, a cartographer he had met on Ixalan. She arrived with ink pots and rolled maps, and together they set out to chart a world nobody had charted yet. {Kellan Joins Up} remembers the moment he chose the gang, and {Getaway Glamer} the moment he sealed the dragon away.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "I found out who I was. Now it's time to discover who I am.",
                        "Kellan, the Kid",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-crew-veteran", "The Veteran",
                    listOf(
                        "Annie Flash is the best sharpshooter on Thunder Junction. Among her own people, the nomadic Atiin, she is called Haseya. Before she ever came to the plane an angel gave her a magic eye, and its iris shines gold when she uses it. It lets her see for miles, look out from other angles, and see straight through illusions. Her left leg is weaker than her right, and she sometimes limps. She once rode a bird and nearly broke her neck, and she has been afraid of flying ever since. She wears braids woven with beaded leather, and her boot fringe is strung with bone and turquoise.",
                        "She has tried to retire more than once. She rode with the Freestriders until Akul's revenge nearly killed her nephew Tommy, and then she quit, took Tommy in, and buried her thunder rifle in the yard of a ranch outside Saddlebrush. Her horned mount {Fortune, Loyal Steed} stayed with her. When Oko turned up on her porch she said no, and then she dug up the rifle, because a man who could find her could be followed by others who would.",
                        "On the card {Annie Flash, the Veteran} she has flash and pulls cards off the top of the library whenever she taps, and {Annie Joins Up} carries the line that sums her up, one last job and then she could retire in peace. During the heist she saved passengers on the train, went back for Kellan when the others would not, and grazed Oko's shoulder with a warning shot in the vault. Afterward she took Kellan in on her ranch. Her look and her people were developed with Navajo cultural consultants, and her Atiin name comes from a Navajo word that may mean to rise up or to be born again.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "One last job, then she could retire in peace.",
                        "Annie Joins Up",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-crew-hired-guns", "The Hired Guns",
                    listOf(
                        "Oko's crew filled the Wildcard Saloon in Rustwood. {Tinybones, the Pickpocket} is a tiny skeleton thief from Urborg on Dominaria who speaks only in clattering, can take himself apart and put himself back together, and picks locks with his own finger bones. He steals because he has a tiny family with a giant appetite. {Breeches, the Blastmaker} is a goblin pirate from Ixalan who blows things up, and {Malcolm, the Eyes} is a siren who watches from the sky. Both once sailed with Vraska on the Belligerent.",
                        "The siblings {Gisa, the Hellraiser} and {Geralf, the Fleshwright} came from Innistrad. Geralf was hired as the crew's medic and brought his sister along as part of a study on what travel between worlds does to magic users. {Eriette, the Beguiler} is a witch from Eldraine whose charms can turn a guard into a friend. {Kaervek, the Punisher} is an ancient warlock of Zhalfir, and {Satoru, the Infiltrator} is the Kamigawan ninja Satoru Umezawa, who was the first of his gang through an Omenpath and was unimpressed by what he found. The two of them had fallen out over something before the job began.",
                        "And then there was the demon. {Rakdos, the Muscle} is the lord of a Ravnican guild, so big he could not fit through the saloon door. He fought Akul at the vault with obvious delight and carried the crew out on his back when it began to collapse. Afterward he left with Tinybones, whom he had grown fond of. When Oko admitted there was no pay, the rest of the crew went their separate ways, saying they would ride with him again for a bigger score or for revenge.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "It was going to be the greatest heist of his unlife.",
                        "Tinybones Joins Up",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-crew-the-client", "The Client",
                    listOf(
                        "The crew's client was Ashiok, the faceless sculptor of nightmares, who spoke in their heads during the job and drew a scholar's memories out of his skull like silver thread. The client was not Ashiok. It was [Jace Beleren](who/jace), believed dead since the invasion, wearing Ashiok's face. Years earlier, while working for the Infinite Consortium, Jace had been sent to Thunder Junction by Tezzeret to look into the vault on behalf of [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas), who was afraid of something inside it. Jace sensed a sleeping, childlike mind behind its walls and could not get in.",
                        "Second in command of the crew was the gorgon {Vraska, the Silencer}, whom most of the Multiverse also believed lost in the invasion. Oko thought he had hired her. In truth she was Jace's partner and had placed herself in the gang on purpose, and she quietly brought Breeches and Malcolm along to back her up. {Vraska Joins Up} says it plainly, letting Oko think he held all the cards was the perfect cover for her own scheme.",
                        "When Oko turned the key, Jace dropped his disguise, shattered a glass pod at the top of the vault and lifted out a small orange furred creature with two dark horns. Vraska told Oko his services were no longer needed and turned her gaze on him, and he escaped with stone already creeping up his neck. Then the two of them were gone with the child. How Jace survived the invasion and what he meant to do next is told in [The Showdown](otj-the-showdown).",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Letting Oko believe he held all the cards provided the perfect cover for her own scheme to unfold.",
                        "Vraska Joins Up",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction"),
                StorySource("Story", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction, Episodes 1 to 6, by Akemi Dawn Bowman, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Story", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction, Epilogues 1 and 2, Bring the End, by Alison Lührs, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Oko"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Kellan"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Annie Flash"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Tinybones"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "otj-the-job", "The Job",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-job-an-offer", "An Offer of Revenge",
                    listOf(
                        "The story opens on the road. Annie Flash was riding through the desert when she came on a stagecoach that Akul and his Hellspurs had torn apart. The courier, a man named Archie Dixon, and two Sterling Company guards were dead in a ring of fire. The dragon had come for a package, and the package had been a decoy, so he had burned everything in his rage. Annie took the lockbox of cash the Hellspurs had left behind and rode home to Saddlebrush with it.",
                        "A well dressed stranger was waiting on her porch. Her angel's eye saw through his disguise at once. He was Oko, he was putting a crew together to rob Akul, and he had come for her because he knew she held a grudge against the dragon and because nobody could fool her eye. If she changed her mind, he said, she would find him at the Wildcard Saloon in Rustwood. She turned him down.",
                        "Then she thought about what she had just learned. If Oko could find her, so could anyone, and the Hellspurs had a long memory. She walked out a hundred paces from the fence, dug up the thunder rifle she had buried there, and saddled Fortune. Across the plane, meanwhile, Ral Zarek had a new bodyguard, a young man from Eldraine with a habit of catching people when they fell. The card {One Last Job} carries the spirit of the episode, an old outlaw pulled back in for one more ride.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-job-jailbreak", "The Jailbreak",
                    listOf(
                        "At the Wildcard, Annie met the crew and learned the real target. It was not Akul's money. It was Maag Taranau, the ancient vault at the heart of the plane, which Annie had always thought was a myth. Ashiok assured her it was real, and that Akul was close to controlling it, but he lacked a key, and one piece of that key sat in Baron Bertram Graywater's safe in Prosperity. Oko meant to steal it and, on the same day, break two of his crew, Kaervek and Satoru Umezawa, out of the Sterling cells beneath the company's headquarters.",
                        "Annie and Eriette handled the cells. They slipped in through a dissolved vent and charmed their way down to the basement, where illusions made the cells look empty. As they freed the warlock and the ninja, Annie realized the gang was full of old grudges and new ones and that the only thing holding it together was the job. Umezawa went to help Oko, and the others left the way they had come. Upstairs, Oko walked into the front office disguised as a courier and had Tinybones delivered to Graywater's office in a cardboard box. Umezawa opened the safe hidden behind a portrait. The key was small, blackened and gleaming with strange fluorescent spots.",
                        "Then, on the road out of town, Oko passed Ral Zarek and his bodyguard. The bodyguard was Kellan, and father and son felt each other's fae magic at the same instant. Oko's disguise fell apart in the street and Sterling guns opened up. He fled as an eagle and Kellan ran him down alone and told him who he was. When Ral and the guards cornered them, the gang arrived, Ral was too stunned at the sight of Vraska to strike, and Rakdos sent him flying with one blow. Oko took the boy with him. {Plan the Heist} is the card of this episode, all schemes and stolen keys.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-job-the-train", "A Train to Prosperity",
                    listOf(
                        "Back at the saloon Kellan recognized Eriette and Ashiok from the trouble on Eldraine, and Oko told him grudges from other planes did not count here. Kaervek studied the key and declared it neither Thran nor Phyrexian, but as old as either or older, and warned that it might be more than a key. Nobody knew where the vault was. Annie knew of an Outcaster scholar of ancient artifacts named Nolan whom the Sterling Company had hired to study the key, and Vraska worried the company would lock him away. He was riding a guarded train into Prosperity.",
                        "Oko promised his son that no innocents would be hurt. Breeches was to blow a bridge to stop the train, and Gisa was to raise zombies aboard as a distraction, with Ashiok's voice keeping everyone in time. Gisa raised her dead too soon, while the train was still moving. The zombies overran the cars, reached the driver's cab and ate the driver, and the train sped up toward the broken bridge. Oko and Kellan crossed the roof disguised as Sterling guards and pulled Nolan out of the prisoner car, and Annie rode alongside to haul the scholar and a wounded Umezawa up onto Fortune.",
                        "Then Oko jumped, and told his son the passengers could not be saved. Kellan stayed. He threw golden vines around the last car and held it while the front of the train hung out over the canyon, which is the moment {Mystical Tether} shows. Annie came back for him and held off the Sterling riders while the passengers climbed out, and then the train fell and burst in the canyon below. At camp Oko claimed he had left because he trusted Kellan to finish the job. Ashiok reached into Nolan's mind and pulled out what they needed, the moment of {Seize the Secrets}. The key was one of six pieces, Akul wore the other five, and a map to his town lay buried in Thief's Folly.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Kellan had no plan. He simply poured his focus into the train and willed it to stop.",
                        "Mystical Tether",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction"),
                StorySource("Story", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction, Episode 1, An Offer of Revenge, by Akemi Dawn Bowman, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Story", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction, Episode 2, The Jailbreak, by Akemi Dawn Bowman, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Story", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction, Episode 3, A Train to Prosperity, by Akemi Dawn Bowman, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Outlaws of Thunder Junction"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Oko"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "otj-the-showdown", "The Showdown",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-showdown-tarnation", "Finding Tarnation",
                    listOf(
                        "Gisa and Geralf came back from Thief's Folly bruised, with the map in hand. It showed that the vault hung in the air over Akul's own town. While they were gone, Annie and Kellan rode Nolan back toward a town, and on the way Annie told the boy some of her past and admitted that she did not trust Oko or Ashiok with whatever was in the vault, but she trusted Akul even less. Oko picked his infiltration team, himself, Vraska, Annie and Kellan, and left the rest of the crew waiting outside the town.",
                        "He made other preparations nobody knew about. He chose Kellan because the boy could not lie, so they would be caught and brought face to face with Akul and his key. He had Tinybones take himself apart and ride along disguised as the bones of Oko's armor. And he sent an anonymous tip about Tarnation's location to the Sterling Company. Tarnation sat in a round canyon beneath a jagged floating mountain, with clouds circling its peak and lava pouring off its edges into rivers that ran through the streets. Its houses were lava rock and sharpened timber framed in animal bones, lit by colored lanterns.",
                        "Within minutes Kellan was too polite to a Hellspur, and a brawl broke out. The four were chained, and when iron cuffs closed on Oko his fae magic failed and the Hellspurs recognized him as the thief from the Graywater job. They were dragged before Akul in an old quarry. The dragon took the stolen piece and slotted it into the medallion around his neck, a disc with five spikes and six glass domes. It grew a sixth spike and the last dome lit an iridescent violet. They had never been six keys. They were six pieces of one.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Akul didn't know what lay inside Tarnation's great vault, but the mystery fueled his obsession.",
                        "Insatiable Avarice",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-showdown-high-moon", "High Moon",
                    listOf(
                        "With nothing left to lose, Kellan challenged the dragon to a duel. Akul laughed at him. Then Oko, Annie and Vraska called him a coward in front of his own gang, and the dragon understood that refusing now would cost him everything he had built. At midnight, under a clock tower that dripped molten rock, he ringed the dueling ground in fire and declared it a fight to the death. Kellan's weapons glanced off his scales.",
                        "Then the fae magic in Kellan woke as it never had before. He put the dragon into a trance, unable to move, and looped a vine around his throat, and found that he could not pull it tight. Akul shook free and pinned him. Before the killing blow could fall, thunder struck the dragon. Ral Zarek and a Sterling army came over the ridge, sent there by Oko's tip, and Tarnation exploded into a gunfight. One of their shots broke the chain that held the key, and in the chaos Tinybones put himself back together and unlocked the prisoners' chains. Vraska turned their guards to stone.",
                        "Kellan threw the key to his father and tried to follow. A blast threw him down and a Sterling guard clubbed him senseless, and the last thing he saw was his father looking at him without interest and walking away toward the vault. He woke in a Sterling prison wagon. Ral tore it open with lightning and made him an offer. Whatever was in that vault, nobody on Thunder Junction could be trusted with it, and Ral meant to carry it somewhere no one would ever find it. Kellan said yes.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "They both knew only one of them would walk away.",
                        "High Noon",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-showdown-the-vault", "Thieves and Thunder",
                    listOf(
                        "The last episode is called The Ballad of Thieves and Thunderslingers. Annie's eye opened the round outer door of the vault and found a grid of deadly light inside, and Umezawa and Tinybones shut it down. Kaervek unpicked a ward on the next door while the rest held the stairs, because Akul had arrived with a fresh gash across his face and what was left of his Hellspurs. Rakdos threw himself at the dragon and beat him. Oko and Vraska climbed on to the inner door, and Kellan and Ral caught them there. Kellan lassoed his father's wrist, Ral and Vraska fought, and Annie grazed Oko's shoulder on purpose.",
                        "Then Ashiok arrived and dropped Kellan, Annie and Ral with nightmares, and Oko turned the key. Inside was endless golden mist and floating stairs that crossed and overlapped, and at the top a glass pod in a metal cage written over with ancient script, under a mural of a vast horned creature. Ashiok let the disguise fall and was [Jace Beleren](who/jace). He broke the pod and lifted out a small sleeping creature with orange fur and two dark horns. Vraska turned on Oko, who barely escaped, and the two of them vanished with the child.",
                        "Jace had set off a trap, and the vault began to fall in. Rakdos carried the crew out. At the last moment Akul seized Annie, and Kellan stopped fighting what he was. He took the dragon's mind completely and ordered him back into the vault, never to come out. Then the mountain shed its stone, and underneath was a golden sphere that shot up into the clouds. It fell back later and smashed across Tarnation, scattering what it held across the plane, and the story never says whether Akul survived. The crew broke up unpaid at the Wildcard, and {Betrayal at the Vault} tells how it ended.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The vault cracked open, and the unlikely alliance met the likeliest of ends, as greed, rage, and self-interest won out over any shred of loyalty.",
                        "Betrayal at the Vault",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-showdown-bring-the-end", "Bring the End",
                    listOf(
                        "Two epilogues by Alison Lührs, titled Bring the End, explain how Jace came to be inside that vault. In the last hours of the invasion, nearly compleated at the foot of the Invasion Tree, he had tried to set off the Filigree Sylex and end everything, and nothing had happened. He woke with Elspeth's sword through his chest and a little of himself restored. He hid behind an illusion, slipped into the mind of [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn), and found her standing order for him. Go home. His Phyrexian body carried him to Vryn, the world he came from, and from somewhere deep in his own mind he fought it back, burned the oil out with a fever and tore the cables from his arms.",
                        "He went to Ravnica and found Vraska compleated on a rooftop, still holding out in a hidden room he had built in her mind long before. He broke her free and carried her to Vryn, to his mother Ranna Beleren, who had believed him dead for decades, and she healed them both over months. There they saw an Omenpath for the first time and agreed the Multiverse was too broken to repair. When Vraska tried to leave and found her spark gone, Jace remembered the vault on the empty desert world and the sleeping mind inside it.",
                        "So he took Ashiok's face. He freed Eriette from a jail on Eldraine, Vraska recruited her old shipmates Breeches and Malcolm on Ixalan, and Oko did the rest without knowing who had hired him. When Jace touched the child's mind after the heist he learned its name was Loot, and that it held a living map of every world and the end of every Omenpath, with planes being born and dying as it watched. The two of them promised to keep Loot safe. What they meant to do with him was the next story, and the one after that.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction"),
                StorySource("Set", "The Big Score"),
                StorySource("Story", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction, Episode 4, Finding Tarnation, by Akemi Dawn Bowman, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Story", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction, Episode 5, High Moon, by Akemi Dawn Bowman, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Story", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction, Episode 6, The Ballad of Thieves and Thunderslingers, by Akemi Dawn Bowman, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Story", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction, Epilogues 1 and 2, Bring the End, by Alison Lührs, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Maag Taranau"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Akul"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "otj-tales-of-the-range", "Tales of the Range",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-tales-no-tells", "No Tells",
                    listOf(
                        "Alongside the main story, Magic Story ran four side tales set on the plane. The first, No Tells by Isaac Fellman, belongs to Yuma, a former Riveteer from New Capenna who can talk to plants. He had been a weaponsmith and an enforcer for the gang, and after the invasion he left it with his friend Elnor, her girlfriend Shadress and a few others to start a cooperative of their own. For a while it went well, well enough to pay for Yuma's medical transition. Then the debts piled up, powerful enemies gathered, and Shadress died.",
                        "Yuma saw three futures, dead, jailed or dragged back into the Riveteers, and took a fourth. He ran through a random Omenpath with all the money the cooperative had left, and it put him down on Thunder Junction. There he found a newborn cactusfolk, a sturdy little cactus the size of a toddler with six stumpy, spined arms, and named him Kirri. He opened a bar, and he brought cocktails, cards and dice to the frontier.",
                        "Elnor followed him through the same path with fire in her hands and a fire breathing snake demon coiled around her arm. Their shootout of fire and thunder set the bar ablaze, until the plane's sudden winter rain came down and drenched it, and Kirri held the building together with interlacing vines and huge red flowers nobody had ever seen. Elnor let bygones be bygones and left to make a new life of her own. Yuma and Kirri went on to lead the Desert Bloom Commander deck as {Yuma, Proud Protector} and {Kirri, Talented Sprout}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "He may be a little green, but trust me, he's far from spineless.",
                        "Yuma, proud protector, Kirri, Talented Sprout",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-tales-venom", "Thicker Than Venom",
                    listOf(
                        "Blood Is Thicker Than Venom, by H. E. Edgmon, is Annie Flash's story before Oko came to her porch. Her nephew Tommy had come to Thunder Junction to live with her after some trouble on their home plane, and he had been a lively, healthy young man until Akul stung him. The venom did not kill him. It left him gaunt and frail, leaning on a cane, bedridden for long stretches, in pain that would not end, and slowly it pushed him away from her and from the life they had tried to build.",
                        "Annie was not entirely alone in Saddlebrush. Jody Carpenter, a widower with silver curls who had come to the plane a year after losing his wife, wanted nothing from her she did not want to give, and teased her gently in the town square the morning after. A basket turned up on her doorstep that he swore he had not left. And when a rattlewurm threatened the town, Annie rounded up a posse to stop it, and Jody came out to see them off.",
                        "In the end Tommy chose to leave. He went home to the Atiin homeland, where there was real medicine and a place he belonged, and he told his aunt he loved her as he went. He left with no ill will, but Annie blamed herself for what Akul had done to him, and that is the grudge Oko knew he could use. The rattlewurm she rode against is the kind shown on {Colossal Rattlewurm}.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-tales-long-way", "A Long Way From Home",
                    listOf(
                        "A Long Way From Home, by Akemi Dawn Bowman, follows Nashi, the young nezumi from Kamigawa who was adopted by the storyteller Tamiyo and lost her in the invasion. After the Omenpaths opened he took up her mission of collecting stories from across the Multiverse, and Thunder Junction was where he began. But his story magic kept failing him. The memory of watching his mother die sat in the way of every spell.",
                        "He heard about the Slickshot Gang's elixir, the drink only Lilah could brew, which made its drinker's magic stronger. He watched their headquarters, broke in, and ran straight into Obeka, the gang's enforcer, an ogre chronologist with an hourglass lantern at her waist who lives only for the moment and for a good fight. She hit him with a punch charged with time magic, and he found himself living through his past all over again. He escaped while still caught in the visions.",
                        "Living through those memories of his mother changed something. Instead of being stopped by them, he was steadied by them, and he came back. This time he cast his story magic through recorded video footage instead of scrolls, a way that suited a Kamigawan tinkerer, and it worked far better. He got the elixir. Lilah's monopoly was broken, and {Obeka, Splitter of Seconds} carries the threat she made with her fists.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "You're living on borrowed time.",
                        "Obeka, Splitter of Seconds",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-tales-family-outing", "A Family Outing",
                    listOf(
                        "A Pleasant Family Outing, by Seanan McGuire, is the trip the necromancer siblings Gisa and Geralf took to Thief's Folly to find the map to Tarnation, after Oko sent them to dig it up. Thief's Folly is the plane's great graveyard, a walled field of tombs from dozens of worlds, and the map had been hidden among them with some care. Gisa, poking at unusual graves, set off a trap.",
                        "The trap was a monstrous zombie centipede, and it chased the twins across the cemetery. It also caught the attention of Stella Lee, an Atiin knife thrower who rode with the Slickshots. She had been working a job, selling fake miracle cures supposedly made from angel's blood, when her friend Lucy Bluff died, and she had missed the funeral. She had come to pay her respects at Lucy's grave, and she did not want it disturbed.",
                        "So Stella helped two strangers she had no reason to like. Between the three of them they broke the necromancy holding the creature together, and the twins dug up the map and went back to the gang with it. Stella went on to lead the Quick Draw Commander deck as {Stella Lee, Wild Card}, carrying a luck granting whip of warm metal she found on the plane, looped in knots that dance with blue thunder. {Geralf, the Fleshwright} and {Gisa, the Hellraiser} went on to Tarnation.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "No Tells, by Isaac Fellman, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Story", "Blood Is Thicker Than Venom, by H. E. Edgmon, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Story", "A Long Way From Home, by Akemi Dawn Bowman, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Story", "A Pleasant Family Outing, by Seanan McGuire, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Yuma"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Nashi"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Stella Lee"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "otj-mechanics", "Mechanics",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-mech-crime", "Committing a Crime",
                    listOf(
                        "In a villain set, you get to be the villain, and the set's central idea is the crime. You commit a crime whenever you cast a spell, activate an ability or put a triggered ability on the stack that targets an opponent, anything an opponent controls, or a card in an opponent's graveyard. It happens the moment the spell or ability goes on the stack. It does not matter whether it resolves, whether it is countered, or whether its targets change later. One spell is one crime, however many things it targets.",
                        "The rule is about targeting, not harm. Untargeted removal like a sweeper is not a crime, while a perfectly kind spell that puts a counter on an opponent's creature technically is. The design was meant to be backward compatible, so a burn spell printed decades ago commits a crime just as well as anything in this set. Most of the set's payoffs trigger only once each turn, because committing several crimes a turn is easy and the designers did not want them stacking out of control.",
                        "The payoffs are everywhere. {Gisa, the Hellraiser} makes two zombies, {Magda, the Hoardmaster} makes a Treasure, {Freestrider Lookout} digs for a land, {Hardbristle Bandit} untaps, {Lazav, Familiar Stranger} grows and copies, and {Omenport Vigilante} gains double strike. {Duelist of the Mind}, the World Champion card for Nathan Steuer, loots when you commit one. Nothing in this set punishes crime. That had to wait for a later Commander card.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-mech-plot", "Plot",
                    listOf(
                        "Plot is the set's scheming mechanic, plot as in plot and scheme. A card with plot has a plot cost. During your main phase with the stack empty you may pay that cost and exile the card from your hand, face up, and it becomes plotted. On any later turn you may cast it from exile without paying its mana cost, again only at sorcery speed. Plotting is a special action, so nobody can respond to stop it, and you can never cast a card on the same turn you plotted it.",
                        "It looks like foretell and it looks like suspend, and it differs from both. Unlike foretell the card sits face up where every opponent can see what you are planning, and unlike suspend there is no clock, the card simply waits until you want it. That opens a lot of play. You can spend spare mana on an early turn so a later turn is free, plot two or three cards to unleash in a single explosion, or hold a creature with an enter trigger until the moment it matters.",
                        "Some plot costs are cheaper than the card's real cost, the price of patience. {Slickshot Show-Off} plots for two and later arrives as a hasty flier, {Stagecoach Security} plots for four and pumps your team when it lands, and {Plan the Heist} draws three cards on a later turn. Other cards plot things that have no plot of their own, like {Make Your Own Luck}, {Kellan Joins Up}, {Lilah, Undefeated Slickshot} and {Fblthp, Lost on the Range}, who lets you plot the top card of your library.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-mech-saddle", "Saddle and Mounts",
                    listOf(
                        "The set brought in a new creature type, Mount, and a keyword that goes with it, saddle. Saddle works a little like crew on a Vehicle. You tap any number of your other creatures with total power at least equal to the saddle number, as a sorcery, and the Mount becomes saddled until end of turn. The difference is that a Mount is already a creature. It can attack and block on its own without anyone riding it. Saddling only makes it better.",
                        "Most Mounts care about attacking while saddled, and that only counts if the Mount was saddled when it was declared as an attacker. {Drover Grizzly} gives your whole team trample, {Calamity, Galloping Inferno} makes attacking copies of the creatures that rode it, and {Fortune, Loyal Steed} flickers itself and its rider at the end of combat. The flavor is the rider climbing on and the pair becoming more than either alone, which is why the Mount gets the bonus and not the rider.",
                        "Saddle was actually first designed for Aetherdrift, where it later returned, as a way to show racing teams riding beasts instead of vehicles. Outlaws of Thunder Junction had its own, more complicated riding mechanic in development, and when the designers noticed two riding mechanics so close together they kept the simpler one and moved it here. There is a rare Mount in each color, {Fortune, Loyal Steed}, {Archmage's Newt}, {Caustic Bronco}, {Calamity, Galloping Inferno} and {Ornery Tumblewagg}, and the uncommon {Miriam, Herd Whisperer} leads the green and white Mounts and Vehicles deck.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-mech-spree", "Spree",
                    listOf(
                        "Spree is a keyword for instants and sorceries that turns one card into several. A spree card has a base mana cost in the corner, and below it two or three modes, each with an additional cost of its own. To cast it you must choose at least one mode and pay its cost, and you may choose as many as you can afford. The spell's mana value is only the base cost, whatever modes you choose, and the modes always happen in the order they are printed.",
                        "That makes a spree card cheap and small early in a game and huge late. {Explosive Derailment} can deal four damage to a creature, destroy an artifact, or both. {Final Showdown} can strip every creature's abilities, protect one of yours, wipe the board, or all three in one go, which is a very Western way to end an argument. {Three Steps Ahead} counters a spell, copies a creature and draws cards, and {Great Train Heist} untaps your team for an extra combat and pays you in Treasure.",
                        "Because spree spells look so different in a fanned hand, the set added a new frame element to mark them, a black triangle with a white plus sign beside the mana cost, and a plus sign before each mode. Neither has any rules meaning. If something lets you cast a spree card without paying its mana cost, you still have to pick a mode and pay for it. Each color has a mythic or rare spree card in a cycle, {Final Showdown}, {Three Steps Ahead}, {Insatiable Avarice}, {Great Train Heist} and {Smuggler's Surprise}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "She'd planned for every eventuality, with the obvious exception of failure.",
                        "Three Steps Ahead",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-mech-outlaws", "Outlaws and Deserts",
                    listOf(
                        "Outlaw is a new word that gathers five creature types under one name, Assassins, Mercenaries, Pirates, Rogues and Warlocks. It is not a creature type itself, so you cannot name it, but cards can refer to an outlaw you control or an outlaw spell. The vision team started with ten candidates and cut them to five because short lists are easier to remember. Barbarian, Berserker, Minion, Ninja and Rebel did not make it. {Rakish Crew}, {Hellspur Posse Boss} and {Vial Smasher, Gleeful Grenadier} are classic outlaw cards.",
                        "Mercenaries got their own token, a 1/1 red Mercenary that can tap to give another creature +1/+0, which many cards in the set make. The set also introduced the creature types Armadillo, Coyote, Mount, Possum and Varmint, saw Beavers and Porcupines on cards for the first time, and folded the old Viashino type into Lizard. It even added a loot counter. There is a pair of red two drops, {Cunning Coyote} and {Resilient Roadrunner}, which has protection from Coyotes, a cartoon chase on cardboard.",
                        "Deserts came back too. A small Deserts theme runs through the set, with cards like {Desert's Due} and {Outcaster Greenblade} that grow stronger for each Desert you control. Ten common dual Desert lands, such as {Lonely Arroyo} and {Creosote Heath}, enter tapped and deal one damage to target opponent when they arrive, so even your lands commit crimes. Some of their flavor text comes from the pioneer journal of a homesteader called Big Ralph.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The sunsets in Spinegully sure make the soul question why we bother with cities.",
                        "Pioneer journal of Big Ralph, Creosote Heath",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-mech-draft", "Ten Ways to Draft",
                    listOf(
                        "Each two color pair in Limited has its own plan, marked by two uncommon gold signposts, one that enables the plan and one legendary creature that rewards it. White and blue plays a plot deck that likes turns where it casts no spells, led by {Jem Lightfoote, Sky Explorer}. Blue and black is crime control with {Lazav, Familiar Stranger}. Black and red is the outlaw deck, red and green leans on big power, and green and white rides Mounts and Vehicles with {Miriam, Herd Whisperer}.",
                        "The enemy pairs are just as distinct. White and black grinds out the long game with {Baron Bertram Graywater}, blue and red wants two or more spells a turn with {Kraum, Violent Cacophony}, black and green recurs creatures with {Honest Rutstein}, red and white runs Mercenary aggression with {Ertha Jo, Frontier Mentor}, and green and blue ramps into big plotted spells with {Doc Aurlock, Grizzled Genius}.",
                        "Play Boosters carried more than the set's own cards. Every pack had a card from the Breaking News sheet, and one pack in five held a card from the List, which for this set was the thirty cards of the Big Score and ten Special Guests. That made opening a pack a small heist of its own, and it made the Limited format unusually full of powerful old spells.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction"),
                StorySource("Story", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction Mechanics, by Matt Tabak, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Crime"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Plot"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Saddle"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Spree"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Outlaw"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "otj-cards", "Cards Worth Knowing",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-cards-legends", "The Legends",
                    listOf(
                        "The set is crowded with legends, which suits a plane where everyone wants to be famous. The crew all have their cards. {Oko, the Ringleader} is the set's planeswalker and the face of its boxes, {Vraska, the Silencer} turns your opponents' dead creatures into your Treasure, {Tinybones, the Pickpocket} casts permanents out of an opponent's graveyard, {Rakdos, the Muscle} plays cards off the top of a library every time you sacrifice something, and {Akul the Unrepentant} cheats creatures from your hand by eating three of your own.",
                        "Visitors from other worlds came along too. {Marchesa, Dealer of Death} cares about crime, {The Gitrog, Ravenous Ride} is a frog Mount that eats its rider to draw cards and drop lands, {Riku of Many Paths} rewards modal spells such as spree, and {Bristly Bill, Spine Sower} grows your team with every land you play. {Roxanne, Starfall Savant} drops Meteorite tokens that make mana and shoot. {Taii Wakeen, Perfect Shot} draws a card whenever a burn spell kills something exactly.",
                        "Then there are the uncommon signpost legends for every color pair, like {Jolene, Plundering Pugilist} from New Capenna, {Ertha Jo, Frontier Mentor} from Zendikar and {Vial Smasher, Gleeful Grenadier} from Tarkir. Each one is built to lead a draft deck, and each is legendary, so each can lead a Commander deck too. {Wylie Duke, Atiin Hero} draws whenever he becomes tapped, and {Kellan, the Kid} plays whole turns for free off the back of a single cast from exile.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-cards-joins-up", "Joining Up",
                    listOf(
                        "One of the set's most loved cycles is the Joins Up rares, five legendary enchantments that show the crew coming together, in mana values that climb from one to five. {Tinybones Joins Up} makes players discard, then pings them whenever a legend arrives. {Vraska Joins Up} hands out deathtouch counters and draws when legends connect. {Kellan Joins Up} plots a cheap card and grows your team for every new legend.",
                        "{Annie Joins Up} deals five damage when it lands and makes your legends' triggered abilities trigger an additional time. {Rakdos Joins Up} brings a creature back from the graveyard with two extra counters and punishes your opponent whenever a legend of yours dies. Each enchantment is itself legendary, and each one carries a small piece of the story in its flavor text.",
                        "The Story Spotlight cards trace the plot in order. {One Last Job} pulls an old outlaw back, {Mystical Tether} shows Kellan stopping the train, {Plan the Heist} lays the scheme, {Seize the Secrets} shows the figure in the scholar's mind, {Betrayal at the Vault} is the double cross, and {Getaway Glamer} is the dragon sealed inside. The key itself appears as {The Key to the Vault}, a legendary Equipment that lets its bearer cast stolen cards for free.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "As the vault collapsed around them, Kellan made sure Akul couldn't follow them out.",
                        "Getaway Glamer",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-cards-staples", "Staples and a Ban",
                    listOf(
                        "Some of the set's cards travelled far beyond Thunder Junction. The enemy colored fast lands from Kaladesh were reprinted at rare, {Concealed Courtyard} among them, and their borderless versions join into one long panorama of the desert. {Terror of the Peaks} was reprinted, as were {Archangel of Tithes} and Snakeskin Veil. {Archmage's Newt} became the first blue creature ever printed that is strictly better than a plain two mana 2/2 bear, making blue the last color to get one, more than six years after Ixalan printed the two before it.",
                        "One card went too far. {This Town Ain't Big Enough} is a five mana instant that returns up to two nonland permanents, and costs three less if one of them is yours. A set later, Bloomburrow printed {Stormchaser's Talent}, and the two made a loop. The bounce spell returned the Class, the Class came back and returned the bounce spell from the graveyard, and the deck could repeat that until the game ended. Standard found it too oppressive, and the card was banned there in June 2025.",
                        "Beyond the main sheet, the set has Showcase Wanted Poster cards for its most notorious characters, a borderless Train Heist panorama, two borderless planeswalkers, and a Buy a Box promo of {Magda, the Hoardmaster}. Open House events gave away a retro {Diabolic Tutor}, and Standard Showdown promos were drawn in the style of the anime Cowboy Bebop, including {Lightning Strike} and {Go for the Throat}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "All that fancy footwork, and you can't even dodge one silly little rope? Cryin' shame, that is.",
                        "This Town Ain't Big Enough",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction"),
                StorySource("Story", "Story Spotlight Cards for Outlaws of Thunder Junction, by Natalie Kreider, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Outlaws of Thunder Junction"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Banned and Restricted Announcement, June 30, 2025"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "otj-the-big-score", "The Big Score",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-big-epilogue", "From Epilogue to Sheet",
                    listOf(
                        "The Big Score is a bonus sheet of thirty cards with its own set code, BIG, and its own symbol, a reinforced vault door. It was released with the main set on April 19, 2024. It was first planned as a separate Epilogue Booster product in the style of March of the Machine, The Aftermath, and when that earlier epilogue was poorly received, the cards were folded into the main set's packs instead. A trace of the old plan survives. Every card on the sheet is legal in Standard.",
                        "The original epilogue had about fifty cards. Thirty became The Big Score, {Jace Reawakened} moved into the main set while {Grand Abolisher} moved onto the sheet in its place, and the rest were never used. That swap means [Jace](who/jace) himself is in the main set, a planeswalker who cannot be cast in your first three turns and plots cards from your hand, which fits a man who had been planning this for a year.",
                        "The cards are the treasures of the vault, everything scattered across the plane when Maag Taranau fell back to the ground. They appear in Play Boosters as part of the List. In Collector Boosters each one also comes in a Vault showcase frame, five of them in raised foil with a golden stamp set into the frame to mark the shiniest loot from Oko's heist, plus extended art versions.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-big-treasures", "Treasures of the Vault",
                    listOf(
                        "The sheet reads like a museum of old Magic, rebuilt by someone who had never seen the originals. {Lotus Ring} shows a Black Lotus on a ring and lets its bearer sacrifice itself for three mana. {Lost Jitte} is a smaller cousin of the famous Umezawa's Jitte. {Memory Vessel} echoes Memory Jar. {Sword of Wealth and Power} is a new Mirran sword, encased by horns shaped like the vault's builders, that gives protection from instants and sorceries and copies your next spell. {Torpor Orb}, first printed in the New Phyrexia set, returns with a new inscription.",
                        "Others are new and strange. {Loot, the Key to Everything} is the child Jace took from the vault, a three color legend whose flavor admits the vault's ultimate prize was not what Kellan had imagined. {Vaultborn Tyrant} is a dinosaur that comes back as an artifact copy when it dies. {Collector's Cage}, {Esoteric Duplicator}, {Worldwalker Helm} and {Oltec Matterweaver} all care about tokens and artifacts. {Fomori Vault} and {Tarnation Vista} are the vault and the view from its town, turned into lands.",
                        "A few cards are pure punishment. {Harvester of Misery} shrinks every other creature on arrival. {Pest Control} sweeps cheap permanents, with Baron Graywater complaining in its flavor about varmints befouling Prosperity's shining streets. {Legion Extruder} shows ancient war machines rumbling to life when the vault door opened. {Omenpath Journey} shows Jace, Vraska and Loot leaving through a path, and sets you off searching your library for five different lands.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The vault's ultimate prize wasn't exactly what Kellan had imagined.",
                        "Loot, the Key to Everything",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-big-fomori", "The Builders",
                    listOf(
                        "Nobody in the story says who built the vault, but the cards leave clues. The art of {Legion Extruder} and of {Nexus of Becoming} shows a strange armored suit that matches the spacesuits of the giant, horned people of the ancient empire that once invaded Ixalan's core. {Worldwalker Helm} is sized for a horned head and made with Ixalan's jade technology. The flavor on {Torpor Orb} is credited as a Fomori inscription, and other cards elsewhere name those people the Fomori of the plane Ir.",
                        "Scholars on other worlds have their own name for them. The loxodon scholar Quintorius Kand called them the Coin Empire after the coin shaped relics they left behind, and on Ixalan they are remembered as the Colonizers. They spread across many planes thousands of years ago and then vanished, leaving vaults like Maag Taranau behind. This fits what Kaervek saw in the key, magic neither Thran nor Phyrexian, as old as either or older.",
                        "The vault's name has its own roots, Old Irish mag for plains and Welsh taranau for thunder, the plain of thunder. Over the ages a skin of rock grew around the real vault until it looked like part of a mountain, and only when it opened did the golden sphere beneath show itself. Inside it slept Loot, under a mural of a vast horned figure. What the Fomori meant him for is a question later stories began to answer.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Devour the hope of a world and it will bow before you.",
                        "Fomori inscription, Torpor Orb",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "The Big Score"),
                StorySource("Set", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction"),
                StorySource("Story", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction, Episode 6, The Ballad of Thieves and Thunderslingers, by Akemi Dawn Bowman, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, The Big Score"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Coin Empire"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Collecting Outlaws of Thunder Junction, by Max McCall, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "otj-breaking-news", "Breaking News",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-news-the-paper", "The Prosperity Post",
                    listOf(
                        "Breaking News is the set's other bonus sheet, sixty five reprinted spells under the code OTP, with a symbol of a jail door with a keyhole. Every card is printed as a newspaper story from the Prosperity Post, each one reporting a crime committed somewhere on the plane, with a headline across the art. It was the first bonus sheet ever to go into Play Boosters, where it took over one of the common slots, so every pack of the set held one.",
                        "The sheet is twenty uncommons, thirty rares and fifteen mythics. In Collector Boosters the fifteen mythics also come as borderless textured foils in an anime style, numbers sixty six to eighty. The cards are not part of the Standard set. They can be played in Limited for this set, and otherwise only in formats where those spells were already legal, so opening one in a draft could hand you a card most of the table had never played with.",
                        "On MTG Arena the sheet was part of the set's drafts, and four of its cards were judged too strong for the Historic format before the set even launched and were banned there in advance. They were {Commandeer}, {Force of Vigor}, {Mana Drain} and {Reanimate}. Force of Vigor was finally released from that ban in February 2026.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-news-headlines", "The Headlines",
                    listOf(
                        "The headlines read like a crime spree across Magic's whole history. Counterspells and theft, {Mana Drain} and {Commandeer} and {Mindslaver}. Murder and robbery, Murder itself and Heartless Pillage. Hand attack, {Thoughtseize}. Removal of every kind, Path to Exile, Leyline Binding, Anguished Unmaking and Vanishing Verse. A handful of old favorites from Ravnica, Abrupt Decay and Detention Sphere, sit beside a {Dust Bowl} to wreck a rival's land.",
                        "Some of the reprints were given new flavor that ties them to the plane. The Breaking News printing of {Cruel Ultimatum} names the Omenport Express, and the {Clear Shot} printing tells of the Thunder Trial. {Fell the Mighty} mentions the Southern Trail. Several cards point at the gangs, with Hellspur crimes behind Bedevil and Fling, Sterling Company business behind Skullcrack and Overwhelming Forces, and the Slickshots behind Ionize.",
                        "And one reprint is a wanted man. {Oko, Thief of Crowns}, the planeswalker who once dominated Standard so thoroughly that he was banned there and in other formats besides, came back on the sheet in newspaper form, with a borderless textured foil version of his own. On a plane where Oko was robbing the biggest vault there was, it was a joke that needed no punchline.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Breaking News"),
                StorySource("Set", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction"),
                StorySource("Story", "A First Look at Outlaws of Thunder Junction, by Clayton Kroh and Adam Styborski, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Breaking News"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Arena Announcements, April 8, 2024"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "otj-commander", "Commander Decks",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-cmd-overview", "Four Decks",
                    listOf(
                        "Four Commander decks came out alongside the set on April 19, 2024, under the code OTC. Corey Bowen led their design. Each deck holds ten new cards, a borderless traditional foil commander, a second foil legend, tokens, a life wheel and a two card Collector Booster sample, along with three bounty cards. These decks ended the run of foil etched display commanders. The face commanders were printed as traditional foil borderless cards instead.",
                        "They also leaned on a cycle of lands. The enemy colored filter lands that had first appeared in the Fallout decks shortly before were reprinted here, and several old staples got new frontier art, {Sol Ring} among them, along with Brainstealer Dragon, Guttersnipe, Impulsive Pilferer, Extract Brain, Fallen Shinobi and Wreck and Rebuild. The Sol Ring's flavor even refers to Thunder Junction.",
                        "The four decks are Desert Bloom, led by Yuma, Quick Draw, led by Stella Lee, Most Wanted, led by Olivia Voldaren, and Grand Larceny, led by Gonti. Between them they cover lands and Deserts, spellslinging, Treasure and outlaws, and playing your opponents' own cards against them. Each also borrows the main set's mechanics, plot and outlaws among them, and a few rarer ones from Magic's past, cipher, convoke, escape, encore, flashback, ninjutsu and storm.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-cmd-desert-bloom", "Desert Bloom",
                    listOf(
                        "Desert Bloom is red, green and white, and it is about putting lands into your graveyard and growing them back. Its commander {Yuma, Proud Protector} is the former Riveteer from the side story No Tells. He costs one less for every land card in your graveyard, can sacrifice a land to draw whenever he enters or attacks, and makes a 4/2 Plant Warrior with reach whenever a Desert hits your graveyard from anywhere.",
                        "His companion {Kirri, Talented Sprout} is the deck's other new legend, the little cactusfolk who grew Yuma's bar back from the ashes, here pumping your Plants and pulling a land back to your hand every turn. The deck's other new cards include {Rumbleweed}, a huge plant that costs one less for each land in your graveyard and gives your team +3/+3 and trample, and {Cactus Preserve}, a Desert that can become a Plant creature as big as your commander.",
                        "Around them sit older cards that love lands in the graveyard, Ramunap Excavator, Titania, Protector of Argoth, Avenger of Zendikar and the Deserts of Amonkhet. The main set lends it {Map the Frontier}, {Conduit Pylons} and three of the new crime Deserts, and {Bovine Intervention} turns a problem into an ox.",
                        "The deck's tagline sums it up, Discard lands, grow them back. A land discarded early is not lost, it is planted, and a Desert that dies comes back as a Plant Warrior with reach. Its three bounties are Lord Fajjal, Paq, Fleeting Filcher and Vara Beth Hannifer. It is the gentlest of the four decks in spirit, a story about a man who ran from his old life and found a new one raising a child in the desert, and the cards play the same way, slow to start and very hard to stop once everything has grown.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "It's a one-ecosystem army.",
                        "Rumbleweed",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-cmd-quick-draw", "Quick Draw",
                    listOf(
                        "Quick Draw is blue and red, and it is about casting several spells a turn and copying your instants and sorceries. Its commander {Stella Lee, Wild Card} is the thrill seeking Atiin knife thrower and Slickshot duelist from the side story A Pleasant Family Outing. Whenever you cast your second spell each turn she exiles the top card of your library for you to play, and once you have cast three spells in a turn she can tap to copy one.",
                        "Its other new legend is {Eris, Roar of the Storm}, a Slickshot elemental who costs less for every different mana value among the instants and sorceries in your graveyard and makes a 4/4 dragon whenever you cast a second spell in a turn. New cards like {Lock and Load} and {Pyretic Charge} carry plot, so the deck can line up an enormous turn in advance.",
                        "The deck's lines are Sling multiple spells and Unleash big turns, and its reprints are a who's who of spellslinging, from Talrand, Sky Summoner and Young Pyromancer to Niv-Mizzet, Parun and Arcane Bombardment. It is a duelist's deck, fast and showy, which is exactly how the Slickshots like to work.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Always bring a little extra thunder.",
                        "Lock and Load",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-cmd-most-wanted", "Most Wanted",
                    listOf(
                        "Most Wanted is red, white and black, and it is about earning Treasure and spending it on outlaws. Its commander is {Olivia, Opulent Outlaw}. The vampire queen of Innistrad had failed to take all vampire power there and had her wedding ruined, so she came to Thunder Junction to clear her head, cause a little chaos and plan her next move. She found a crowd of frustrated cast offs, and when she spoke they listened.",
                        "On the card she flies with lifelink, makes a Treasure whenever your outlaws connect, and turns two Treasures into counters across your whole team. Its other new legend, {Vihaan, Goldwaker}, gives your other outlaws vigilance and haste and can turn your Treasures into 3/3 Assassin constructs for combat. New cards include {Angelic Sell-Sword}, an angel who hires a Mercenary for every creature that joins you, and {Bounty Board}, which puts prices on heads.",
                        "The tagline is Crime always pays, and the deck is full of old pirates and assassins, Captain Lannery Storm, Queen Marchesa, Mari, the Killing Quill and Massacre Girl among them. {Graywater's Fixer} gives every outlaw in your graveyard encore, so the gang can ride again.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-cmd-grand-larceny", "Grand Larceny",
                    listOf(
                        "Grand Larceny is black, green and blue, and it is about stealing. Its commander {Gonti, Canny Acquisitor} is the aetherborn crime lord from Avishkar, who wrangled a post as one of their plane's emissaries and set up an office on Thunder Junction, working on two projects at once, Avishkar's interests and Gonti's. On the card Gonti exiles the top card of a player's library whenever your creatures connect and lets you play it, and makes spells you do not own cheaper.",
                        "Its other new legend is {Felix Five-Boots}, an ooze rogue who makes your combat damage triggers happen twice, and whose flavor notes that having multiple pseudopods and no vital organs makes dueling a lot easier. New cards include {Smirking Spelljacker}, which steals a spell off the stack and casts it later, {Thieving Varmint}, which makes mana only for spells you do not own, and {Arcane Heist}, which casts a spell from an opponent's graveyard and encodes itself to do it again.",
                        "The tagline is Steal cards, generate mana, and the reprints include Hostage Taker, Thief of Sanity, Villainous Wealth and Gonti's older card, Gonti, Lord of Luxury. The only main set card in the deck is {Doc Aurlock, Grizzled Genius}, whose discount on spells cast from exile fits a deck that lives there.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otj-cmd-bounties", "Bounties",
                    listOf(
                        "The decks brought a new way to play, bounties. Each deck has three bounty cards, twelve in all, each showing a villain wanted somewhere on the plane, with an alternate card back so they cannot be shuffled into a deck. Players who use them share a bounty deck of at least six different bounties, and as the starting player's third turn begins, the top one is revealed.",
                        "Each bounty names a condition, and the player whose turn it is can claim it by meeting that condition. The bounty on Squeakers the Sly pays out if a legendary creature of yours attacked alone. Only one reward can be claimed in a turn. A reward claimed the turn the bounty went up is a single Treasure, and every turn it goes unclaimed the price rises, up to two Treasures and a card at the fourth level.",
                        "When a bounty is claimed it is set aside and the next one goes up. If the bounty deck runs out, there has been a jailbreak, and every claimed bounty is shuffled back in. The wanted include Frankie the Fang and Gorra Tash and Silas of the Hellspurs, Lyssa of the Sterling Company, Miron Tillas Jr. of the Freestriders, Paq of the Slickshots, Sleepy Sovka, Vara Beth Hannifer, Lord Fajjal, Eriana, Rissa Lee and a figure called the Outsider.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction Commander"),
                StorySource("Story", "No Tells, by Isaac Fellman, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Outlaws of Thunder Junction Commander"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction Commander Decklists, by Clayton Kroh, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
